course: dictionary
lesson: 8946
title: Silky: soft, smooth and clever
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Silky first means smooth, soft and shiny, like silk cloth. Look at «Peel back the husk and each long silky thread leads to one kernel.» A thread is long and thin, so silky tells you it is smooth and soft to touch and shines a little.
explain: Silky can also describe how someone moves or speaks. In sport, «silky skills» means smooth, clever moves with the ball, where nothing looks rough or clumsy. A «silky voice» is smooth and gentle, but the story may tell you more. Read the words around it to find out.
explain: Context decides the meaning. If a story says a villain has a silky voice while he plans something bad, silky means falsely sweet. Always ask what the words around it suggest, then try your meaning in the sentence and check it still makes sense.
fact: Silky means smooth, soft and shiny, like silk.
fact: Silkier and silkiest compare things: silky, silkier, silkiest.
fact: In sport, «silky skills» means smooth, clever moves with the ball.
fact: A «silky voice» can be gentle, or falsely sweet when a villain is hiding something.
fact: «Silkies» are hens with five toes, and the name comes from the word silky.
exercise: «Each long silky thread leads to one kernel.» What does silky mean here? | Hot and sticky | *Smooth, soft and shiny | Dark and heavy | Rough and prickly
exercise: In sport, «silky skills» means what? | *Smooth, clever moves with the ball | Slow and heavy | Loud and clumsy | Angry shouts from the crowd
exercise: Which word is the comparative, meaning more silky? | silkiness | silk | *silkier | silkiest
test: «The hairs can float in the air near their silky nests.» In this sentence, silky tells you the nests look: | Hard as stone | Dark and damp | Sticky and cold | *Smooth and soft
test: In a story, a villain has a «silky voice» while he plans a trick. What does silky suggest? | *Smooth and gentle, but falsely sweet | Loud and rough | Squeaky like a toy | Quiet because of a cold
exam: Which is the best check when you meet a new word like silky? | Ignore the sentence and guess | Read the sentence aloud once and stop | Look only at the title | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
